'''Kenneth J. Block''' (born October 11, 1965) is an American businessman, [[software engineer]], and [[political reformer]]. He is the founder of the [[Moderate Party of Rhode Island]], the state's third-largest political party, and ran as the Moderate candidate for [[Governor of Rhode Island]] in the [[Rhode Island gubernatorial election, 2010|2010 election]]. He ran for the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nomination in the 2014 gubernatorial election, losing to [[Cranston, Rhode Island|Cranston]] mayor [[Allan Fung]], and is currently an [[Independent politician|independent]] candidate for governor in the [[2026 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|2026 election]]. He also served as president of RI Taxpayers, a [[taxpayer groups|taxpayer advocacy]] and [[List of consumer organizations|watchdog]] group, until his resignation in May 2013 to mount a campaign for governor.

While leader of the Moderate Party, Block was an advocate for economic, educational, and political reforms in Rhode Island. His court battle to gain official state recognition for the Moderates in 2009 amended several laws he claimed were [[unconstitutional]], and made it easier for [[Third party (United States)|third parties]] to gain signatures and access election ballots.<ref>{{Cite court|litigants=KENNETH BLOCK; MODERATE PARTY OF RHODE ISLAND; and MODERATE PARTY OF RI PAC, Plaintiffs, v. A. RALPH ROLLIS, Secretary of the State of Rhode Island, in his official capacity; ROBERT KANDO, Executive Director of the Rhode Island Board of Elections, in his official capacity; and PATRICK LYNCH, Attorney General of the State of Rhode Island, in his official capacity, Defendants.|opinion=C.A. No. 09-047 S|court=UNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT DISTRICT OF RHODE ISLAND|date=May 29, 2009|url=https://ecf.rid.uscourts.gov/cgi-bin/show_public_doc?2009cv0047-22}}</ref> In a highly contested four-way election, Block received 6.5% of the vote in the race.

Block emerged as one of the most outspoken critics of the [[Rhode Island General Assembly]] in the aftermath of the 2010 elections.<ref name="Nagle"/> He publicly opposed the controversial $75 million loan and subsequent taxpayer bailout of [[38 Studios]],<ref name="Joystiq"/> the taxpayer-funded redevelopment the [[Superman Building]], and introduction of the [[Sakonnet River Bridge]] [[Toll bridge|toll]].<ref name="Chartier"/> Block has also called for the elimination of the "[[master lever]]" from election ballots which he argues creates [[voter disenfranchisement]] and unfairly benefits larger political parties.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Block |first=Kenneth |date=Feb 6, 2013 |title=Ken Block: The Master Lever Challenge to RI Legislators |url=https://www.golocalprov.com/politics/ken-block-the-master-lever-challenge-to-ri-legislators |url-status=live |access-date=April 24, 2026 |website=Go Local Prov}}</ref> He eventually created MasterLever.org to explain his position and enable the public to contact state officials via [[e-mail]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Block |first=Kenneth |title=Eliminate the Master Lever in Rhode Island! |url=masterlever.org |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130201022709/https://masterlever.org/ |archive-date=Feb 1, 2013 |access-date=April 24, 2026 |website=masterlever.org}}</ref>

As a small business owner, Block has run several software companies which have worked with state governments to identify [[welfare fraud|fraud]] and [[government waste]] in [[social service]] and [[welfare spending|welfare]] programs. In 1995, Block was able to save the [[Government of Texas|state of Texas]] over $1 billion by developing a new statewide [[debit card]] system.<ref name="RILPB"/><ref name="PolitiFact"/><ref name="DeQuattro"/> Shortly after the 2010 election, Block offered his company's help free of charge to find waste and fraud in Rhode Island's entitlement programs. He was subsequently involved in the state's [[Medicare (United States)|Medicare]] waste and fraud report as well as assisting the [[List of law enforcement agencies in Rhode Island|Providence Police Department]]'s fraud unit to better access state records in late 2012. Police investigators have claimed that the new computer software will help detect millions of dollars in fraud.<ref name="Estaphan"/>

== Early life ==
Kenneth J. Block was born and raised in [[Milford, Connecticut]]. The oldest of three children, Block's family lived in the same house as his grandparents. He graduated from [[Milford High School (Connecticut)|Milford High School]] in 1983 and then attended [[Dartmouth College]] where he earned a [[bachelor's degree]] in [[computer science]] four years later.<ref>{{cite news |last=Edgar |first=Randal |date=February 22, 2010 |title=Block announces run for governor |page=A1 |work=[[Providence Journal]] }}</ref> Block began his career writing software for [[Wall Street]] [[trading desk]]s during the late 1980s. He was employed by the [[Bank of New England]] and worked at small software consultancy firms Dalcomp Corporation and LOBB Systems.{{Citation needed|date=April 2026}} In 1991, Block moved to Rhode Island to work for the Providence-based [[GTECH Corporation]]. Although initially a one-year consultancy, he stayed with the company for another six years. From 1995 to 1999, Block was president of Kinetic Consulting, Inc., a software consultation firm that worked with state agencies to identify government waste and abuse as well as improving the overall efficiency of their spending programs, until the company was closed during the [[dot-com bust]] period.<ref name="Nagle">{{cite web |url=http://www.golocalprov.com/news/ken-block-to-run-for-rhode-island-governor-as-republican/ |title=Ken Block to Run for Rhode Island Governor as Republican |author=Nagle, Kate |date=October 28, 2013 |website=GoLocalProv.com |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref>

In 2001, Block founded Simpatico Software Systems, a [[data mining]] service used by government agencies and private businesses to track waste and fraud.<ref name="Bashkatova">{{cite news |title=Ken Block enters Republican gubernatorial primary |author=Bashkatova, Mariya |url=http://www.browndailyherald.com/2013/11/05/ken-block-enters-republican-gubernatorial-primary/ |newspaper=[[The Brown Daily Herald]] |date=November 5, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> Additionally, it supplies software services to the [[Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program|SNAP program]]. Similar to his first company, Simpatico Systems also provided engineering and consulting services. Its clients have included the EVERTEC and GTECH Corporations, [[Northrop Grumman]], and the [[Government of Texas|state government of Texas]]. In Texas, Block's company developed a statewide [[debit card]] system for [[Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program|food stamp]] and [[welfare spending|welfare]] recipients that saved the state more than $1 billion.<ref name="RILPB">{{cite web |url=http://webserver.rilin.state.ri.us/News/pr1.asp?prid=7411 |title=Baldelli Hunt: Ken Block volunteer offer good news for R.I. |author=Rhode Island General Assembly |date=June 10, 2011 |publisher=Rhode Island Legislative Press Bureau |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref><ref name="PolitiFact">{{cite web |url=https://www.politifact.com/rhode-island/statements/2010/aug/29/kenneth-block/block-says-system-he-helped-create-detect-welfare-/ |title=Block says system he helped create to detect welfare fraud has saved more than $1 billion |author=Emery, C. Eugene Jr. |date=August 29, 2010 |website=[[PolitiFact.com]] |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref><ref name="DeQuattro">{{cite web |url=http://www.abc6.com/story/23804398/ken-block-to-leave-moderate-party-and-become-a-republican |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131109193938/http://www.abc6.com/story/23804398/ken-block-to-leave-moderate-party-and-become-a-republican |url-status=dead |archive-date=November 9, 2013 |title=Ken Block leaves Moderate Party, becomes a Republican |author=DeQuattro, Dee |date=October 28, 2013 |website=[[WLNE-TV|ABC6.com]] |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> Two years later he created a second company, Cross Alert Systems, which manufactures [[intelligent transportation system|"intelligent" traffic systems]] for [[recreational trail]] and [[public road]] [[intersection]]s.<ref name="Benton">{{cite news |title=Candidates make appeal for Jamestowners' votes |author=Benton, Holly |url=http://www.jamestownpress.com/news/2010-10-28/Front_Page/Candidates_make_appeal_for_Jamestowners_votes.html |newspaper=Jamestown Press |date=October 28, 2010 |access-date=October 1, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150317094835/http://www.jamestownpress.com/news/2010-10-28/Front_Page/Candidates_make_appeal_for_Jamestowners_votes.html |archive-date=March 17, 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="Gregg">{{cite news |title=Ken Block, Moderate Party's 2010 candidate for governor, seeking GOP nomination in 2014 |author=Gregg, Katherine |url=http://www.providencejournal.com/breaking-news/content/20131027-ken-block-moderate-partys-2010-candidate-for-governor-seeking-gop-nomination-in-2014.ece |newspaper=[[The Providence Journal]] |date=October 28, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref>

== Political career ==
{{main|Moderate Party of Rhode Island}}
In 2007, Block decided to enter the [[Politics of Rhode Island|Rhode Island political scene]]. In later interviews, Block said that he entered politics out of frustration with the state government's inability to solve Rhode Island's declining economy and education system.<ref name="Gregg"/> He was particularly concerned that his children were not getting a quality education in [[State school|public school]]s and, like many young Rhode Islanders, would eventually have to leave the state due to high unemployment.<ref name="Baron"/><ref name="Bickford">{{cite web |url=http://www.eastbayri.com/news/barringtons-ken-block-has-a-new-party/ |title=Barrington's Ken Block has a new party |author=Bickford, Josh |date=November 11, 2013 |website=EastBayRI.com |access-date=October 1, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131111202643/http://www.eastbayri.com/news/barringtons-ken-block-has-a-new-party/ |archive-date=November 11, 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref> He also noted the difficulty for small business owners to operate in the state and that the high tax rate forced many companies to relocate to Massachusetts and elsewhere.<ref name="Bashkatova"/> As a longtime business owner himself, Block believed many of these problems could easily be fixed though basic [[fiscal responsibility]] and "[[common sense]]" leadership.<ref name="Mikolay">{{cite web |url=https://ivn.us/2013/05/23/ken-block-hopes-to-represent-centrists-in-rhode-island/ |title=Dennis Mikolay: Ken Block Hopes to Represent Centrists in Rhode Island |author=Mikolay, Dennis |date=May 23, 2013 |website=IVN.us |access-date=October 1, 2013|author-link=Dennis Mikolay }}</ref><ref name="Little">{{cite interview|last=Block |first=Ken |interviewer=Patrick Little |title=Coffee with the Candidates: Ken Block |url=http://www.foxprovidence.com/rhode-show/coffee-with-the-candidates-ken-block-for-governor |publisher=[[WNAC-TV]] |location=Providence, Rhode Island |date=October 1, 2013 |work=The Rhode Show |access-date=October 1, 2013 }}{{dead link|date=May 2017 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

Disillusioned with both the Democratic and Republican parties, who he felt were more concerned battling over [[social issues]], Block believed that starting a [[centrist]]-based third-party would be the best option.<ref name="Bickford"/><ref name="Smith">{{cite news |title=Moderate Party withers in R.I., like other parties |author=Smith, Michelle R. |url=http://www.thewesterlysun.com/news/state/2758842-129/moderate-party-withers-in-ri-like-other-parties.html |newspaper=[[The Westerly Sun]] |date=October 30, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013 }}{{Dead link|date=November 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> His political affiliation is unknown before 2007, however, Block has stated his admiration for former RI Governor [[Bruce Sundlun]].<ref name="WPRI-Newsmakers">{{cite interview|last=Block |first=Ken |interviewer=Tim White and Ted Nesi |title=Newsmakers 11/15: Ken Block, Republican candidate for governor |url=http://www.wpri.com/target-12/tim-white/newsmakers-1115-ken-block-republican-candidate-for-governor |publisher=[[WPRI-TV]] |location=[[Providence, Rhode Island]] |date=November 15, 2013 |work=Newsmakers |access-date=October 1, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131118174445/http://www.wpri.com/target-12/tim-white/newsmakers-1115-ken-block-republican-candidate-for-governor |archive-date=November 18, 2013 }}</ref> A [[Brown University]] political poll reported that 74% of Rhode Islanders were dissatisfied with the state's political leadership and "would be supportive of a new moderate political party". A poll run by Block produced similar numbers. He reasoned that if his party could attract enough moderate voters, they would be able to put pressure on then Republican Governor [[Donald L. Carcieri]] and the Democrat-controlled [[Rhode Island General Assembly|General Assembly]] to address vital issues he felt were being neglected by the state.<ref>{{cite news|title=Moderate Party touted as an antidote for RI's woes |author=Donnis, Ian |url=http://thephoenix.com/boston/news/59593-moderate-party-touted-as-an-antidote-for-ris-woes/ |newspaper=[[The Phoenix (newspaper)|The Phoenix]] |date=April 9, 2008 |access-date=October 1, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161101042543/http://thephoenix.com/boston/news/59593-moderate-party-touted-as-an-antidote-for-ris-woes/ |archive-date=November 1, 2016 }}</ref>

The [[Moderate Party of Rhode Island]] was founded by Block a year later. An official website was launched in early-2008 and several candidates were endorsed in the general election - Jean Ann Guliano (D), John Pagliarini (R), Matt McHugh (I), and Christopher Little (I). On October 23, 2008, Block co-hosted [[Operation Clean Government]]'s ''[[State of the State (TV series)|State of the State]]'' with former RI attorney general [[Arlene Violet]] interviewing all four candidates.<ref>{{cite interview|last=Guiliano |first=Jean Ann |last2=Pagliarini |first2=John |last3=McHugh |first3=Matt |last4=Little |first4=Christopher |interviewer=Block, Ken and [[Arlene Violet]] |title=Interview with Jean Ann Guiliano, John Pagliarini, Matt McHugh, and Christopher Little |url=http://moderate-ri.org/news/state_ofthestate.htm |publisher=[[Cox Communications]] |location=[[Providence, Rhode Island|Providence]] |date=October 23, 2008 |work=[[State of the State (TV series)|State of the State]] |access-date=October 1, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090105212452/http://moderate-ri.org/news/state_ofthestate.htm |archive-date=January 5, 2009 }}</ref> Gaining official recognition by the state was "a fight and a half" according to Block. In February 2009, Block and [[American Civil Liberties Union]] filed a lawsuit against the state to gain official party status.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.moderate-ri.org/news/lawsuit.htm |title=The Rhode Island ACLU has today filed a federal lawsuit against Rhode Island elections officials on behalf of the Moderate Party of Rhode Island (MPRI) |author=Moderate Party of Rhode Island |date=February 3, 2009 |website=Moderate-RI.org |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100311191000/http://www.moderate-ri.org/news/lawsuit.htm |archive-date=March 11, 2010 |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> U.S. District Judge William Smith, striking down several ballot access laws as [[unconstitutional]], ruled in the Moderate Party's favor three months later. The Moderate Party were able to collect over 34,000 signatures, the 12th highest number of signatures in the country, to qualify as a political party and were placed on the 2010 ballot.<ref name="Mikolay"/> Afterwards, Block said that Rhode Island was the most difficult state to start a political party.<ref name="Nagle"/><ref name="Little"/> The Moderate Party would find support from a number of prominent figures including former [[Hasbro]] [[CEO]] [[Alan G. Hassenfeld]], Arlene Violet, and former U.S. Attorney [[Robert Clark Corrente]], the latter briefly serving as party chairman.<ref name="Smith"/><ref name="Marcelo">{{cite news |title=Political Scene: New Republican Ken Block decides that third way is no way in R.I. politics |author=Marcelo, Philip |url=http://www.providencejournal.com/breaking-news/content/20131103-political-scene-new-republican-ken-block-decides-that-third-way-is-no-way-in-r.i.-politics.ece |newspaper=The Providence Journal |date=November 3, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref>

=== 2010 election ===
{{main|2010 Rhode Island gubernatorial election}}

Block was the Moderate Party's candidate for Governor during the [[Rhode Island gubernatorial election, 2010|2010 election]]. He had not intended to run for office, however, Block reluctantly stepped in after failing to find a suitable candidate in time.<ref name="Gregg"/> Block had developed an interest in civics and government while at [[Dartmouth College]] in the 1980s though he had no political experience prior to the campaign. He was successful in gaining the necessary signatures 1,000 registered voters to get on the ballot. His door-to-door campaigning in [[South Kingstown]] was included in a feature story for ''Rhode Island Monthly''.<ref>{{cite journal|author=Liberman, Ellen|date=October 2010|title=Vacancies in top posts, anti-incumbency fever and a new third party have created an election field like none other in Rhode Island this year. Place your November bets.|url=http://www.rimonthly.com/Rhode-Island-Monthly/October-2010/Reporter/|journal=Rhode Island Monthly|publisher=Rhode Island Monthly Communications, Inc.|access-date=2014-01-10|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304051920/http://www.rimonthly.com/Rhode-Island-Monthly/October-2010/Reporter/|archive-date=2016-03-04|url-status=dead}}</ref> Block was one of the candidates profiled on [[Fox Providence]]'s ''The Rhode Show'' for its "Coffee with the Candidates" segment.<ref name="Little"/>

Block finished fourth in the statewide general election with 22,146 votes, garnering 6.5% of the vote.<ref name="DeQuattro"/><ref name="Gregg"/> He followed Democrat [[Frank T. Caprio|Frank Caprio]] with 78,896 votes (23%), Republican John Robitaille with 114,911 votes (33.6%), and the winner, Independent [[Lincoln Chafee]] with 123,571 votes (36.1%).<ref name="Nagle"/> Though an impressive performance for a first-time candidate,<ref name="Mikolay"/> Block was blamed for his part in the four-way race that allowed Chafee to win the election. The Republican Party of Rhode Island was particularly critical of Block for [[Vote splitting|splitting the conservative vote]],<ref name="Baron">{{cite news|title=Block: RI must be fixed up |author=Baron, Jim |url=http://www.pawtuckettimes.com/content/block-ri-must-be-fixed |archive-url=https://archive.today/20140228013508/http://www.pawtuckettimes.com/content/block-ri-must-be-fixed |url-status=dead |archive-date=February 28, 2014 |newspaper=[[Pawtucket Times]] |date=December 3, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013 |quote=In 2010, running under the Moderate Party banner, he finished fourth in a crowded field with about 6.5 percent of the vote. In 2014, he faces a Republican primary against Cranston Mayor Allan Fung. That primary will be made more difficult because many Republicans feel Block was a spoiler in 2010, taking just enough votes away from Republican John Robitaille to allow then-independent Lincoln Chafee to squeak to victory. }}</ref><ref name="WPRI-Newsmakers"/> as Robitaille lost to the more liberal Chafee by less than 4% of the vote.<ref name="DeQuattro"/>

=== Post-election ===
Block continued his political activism as leader of the Moderate Party, stressing the need for economic, educational, and governmental reforms.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.lincolninst.edu/news-events/news-listing/articletype/articleview/articleid/2164/rhode-island-ranked-least-tax-friendly-state-for-retirees |title=Rhode Island Ranked Least Tax-Friendly State for Retirees |author=Nagle, Kate |date=August 30, 2013 |publisher=[[Lincoln Institute of Land Policy]] |access-date=October 1, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160412211841/http://www.lincolninst.edu/news-events/news-listing/articletype/articleview/articleid/2164/rhode-island-ranked-least-tax-friendly-state-for-retirees |archive-date=April 12, 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref> He made several guest appearances on [[WPVD-TV|Rhode Island PBS]]'s public affairs television program ''A Lively Experiment''<ref>{{Cite episode |title=A Lively Experiment |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nHIdoO4yS8w |access-date=October 1, 2012 |first=Dyana (host) |last=Koelsch |network=[[PBS]] |station=[[WSBE-TV]] |location=[[Providence, Rhode Island]] |date=August 10, 2012 }}</ref><ref name="ALE2">{{Cite episode |title=A Lively Experiment |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bHl_xX9JGBI |access-date=October 1, 2012 |first=Dyana (host) |last=Koelsch |network=PBS |station=WSBE-TV |location=Providence, Rhode Island |date=April 5, 2013 }}</ref> and [[Operation Clean Government]]'s ''[[State of the State (TV series)|State of the State]]''. He began a movement to abolish the "master lever", and worked with the state to uncover fraud and waste in the state's welfare programs,<ref name="Nagle" /> going so far as to offer his company's services to Governor Chafee at no charge.<ref name="DeQuattro" /> In January 2013, Block began teaching a technology and business class as an [[adjunct professor]] at the [[University of Rhode Island]].<ref>{{Cite book |title=URI Undergraduate and Graduate Course Catalog 2013-2014, URI Course Catalogs. Book 66 |publisher=University of Rhode Island |year=2013 |pages=298}}</ref>

An outspoken critic of the [[Rhode Island General Assembly]], he challenged a number of controversial decisions made by the legislative body.<ref name="Nagle"/> While running for Governor, Block criticized the state's $75 million loan to [[38 Studios]], in exchange for the [[video game company]] moving its headquarters from [[Maynard, Massachusetts]] to Providence, Rhode Island, suggesting that the money would be better spent investing in local businesses.<ref name="Joystiq">{{cite web |url=http://www.joystiq.com/2010/08/11/rhode-island-politicians-speak-out-against-38-studios-deal/ |title=Rhode Island politicians speak out against 38 Studios deal |author=McElroy, Griffin |date=August 11, 2010 |website=[[Joystiq]].com |access-date=October 1, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131110182048/http://www.joystiq.com/2010/08/11/rhode-island-politicians-speak-out-against-38-studios-deal/ |archive-date=November 10, 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref> He was also strongly opposed to taxpayer-funded redevelopment of the [[Superman Building]]<ref name="Chartier">{{cite web |url=https://oceanstatecurrent.com/analysis/ken-block-not-one-taxpayer-dime-to-the-superman-building/ |title=Ken Block: Not One Taxpayer Dime To The Superman Building |author=Chartier, Monique |date=May 2, 2013 |website=[[Ocean State Current]] |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> and the introduction of the [[Sakonnet River Bridge]] [[Toll bridge|toll]].<ref name="Bickford"/> Block called this most recent legislation by the assembly an "awful decision" and condemned the [[Rhode Island Democratic Party]] as "[proving] itself completely incapable of governing the state."<ref name="Gregg"/>

In November 2020, Block was hired by the [[Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign|presidential campaign]] of [[Donald Trump]] to find [[2020 United States presidential election#False claims of fraud|evidence of election fraud]]. Block wrote a book about his experiences titled ''Disproven'', which was published March 12, 2024.<ref>{{Cite web| last = Crandall| first = Brian| title = Ken Block's book about his voter fraud probe for Trump hits shelves| work = WJAR| access-date = 2024-04-06| date = 2024-03-12| url = https://turnto10.com/politics/ken-blocks-book-about-his-voter-fraud-probe-trump-hits-disproven-votes-numbers-shelves-southern-new-england-rhode-island-politician-election-president-march-12-2024}}</ref>

=== Combating fraud and government waste ===
Shortly after the 2010 election, Democratic State Representative [[Lisa Baldelli-Hunt]] urged Governor Lincoln Chafee to contact Block about working with state agencies to uncover fraud in Rhode Island's [[Medicaid]] programs.<ref name="PJ-Jun2011">{{cite news |last=Gregg |first=Katherine |last2=Marcelo |first2=Philip |last3=Edgar |first3=Randal |date=June 20, 2011 |title=POLITICAL SCENE: Citizens' group critical of pension-panel makeup |page=A2 |work=[[Providence Journal]]}}</ref> In April 2011, Block volunteered his company's services free of charge to help the state identify waste and fraud in Health and Human Services spending.<ref name="Mikolay"/> He was not able to testify in person before the House and Senate Finance Committee that month; a written statement was submitted into the record instead.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.independentpoliticalreport.com/2011/04/rhode-island-ken-block-wants-to-find-ri-hhs-fraud-waste/ |title=Rhode Island: Ken Block wants to find RI-HHS fraud & Waste |author=Montoni, Marc |date=April 13, 2011 |website=Independentpoliticalreport.com |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> {{Unreliable source?|sure=y|reason=News aggregator with no editorial policy.|date=September 2019}} Two months later, Block talked with both the Department of Human Services and the Department of Labor and Training about using his company's services free of charge.<ref name="RILPB"/> After several months negotiating with the Chafee administration, Block announced on [[WPRO (AM)|WPRO]]'s ''The John DePetro Show'' that his company was close to reaching an agreement to start working with state agencies to investigate waste and abuse in Rhode Island's welfare system.<ref>{{cite interview|last=Block |first=Ken |interviewer=[[John DePetro]] |title=Block: Agreement with Chafee administration almost official |url=http://wpro-am.fimc.net/Article.asp?id=2323066&spid=37719 |publisher=WPRO |location=Providence, Rhode Island |date=October 31, 2011 |work=The John DePetro Show |access-date=October 1, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140301162511/http://wpro-am.fimc.net/Article.asp?id=2323066&spid=37719 |archive-date=March 1, 2014 }}</ref>

Block's company, Simpatico Software Systems, began working with the [[List of law enforcement agencies in Rhode Island|Providence Police Department]]'s fraud unit in late 2012. Simpatico Systems developed a computer program so that fraud investigators can more easily access state records. The unit's two investigators, Jack Costa and Daniel Murphy, had previously been dependent on more than a dozen other agencies throughout the state and records were often unavailable in the current system. The Simpatico system specifically assisted the Providence Housing Authority in sharing data with the state government. Their efforts were featured on the November 6th edition of [[WPRI]]'s ''Eyewitness News''. It was claimed that the new computer software would help investigators detect millions of dollars in fraud.<ref name="Estaphan">{{cite AV media|people=Estaphan, Nicole (Host) |date=November 6, 2012 |title=Fraud unit working to protect RI taxpayers |url=http://www.wpri.com/news/local/providence/providence-fraud-unit-working-to-protect-ri-taxpayers |medium=Television |location=Providence, Rhode Island |publisher=WPRI }}{{dead link|date=May 2017 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> He was later involved in the Rhode Island Health and Human Services Waste and Fraud Report which submitted its preliminary findings in early 2013.<ref name="Nagle"/><ref name="RSP">{{cite AV media |people=St. Pierre, Ron and Kate Nagle (hosts) |date=October 28, 2013 |title=Ken Block to Run for Rhode Island Governor as Republican |medium=[[Radio]] |url=https://www.920whjj.com/media/podcast-ron-st-pierres-podcasts-ronstpierre/ken-block-to-run-for-rhode-23894168/ |access-date=October 1, 2013 |location=Providence, Rhode Island |publisher=[[WHJJ]] }}</ref>

=== Ban the Master Lever initiative ===
Another reform pushed by Block was the elimination of [[straight-ticket voting|straight-ticket]] or "master lever" voting, which allows voters to cast a ballot for all candidates of a single political party with one vote, arguing that it confuses and [[voter disenfranchisement|disenfranchises voters]]. The master lever was first introduced to Rhode Island in 1939 when voting machines required voters to physically pull multiple levers to cast a vote. These machines were eventually replaced with a modern electronic system in the late 1990s, yet the law remained in place.<ref name="Kalunian-ML">{{cite news |title=RI considers ban on 'master lever' |author=Kalunian, Kim |url=https://www.warwickonline.com/stories/Move-on-to-ban-master-lever-single-party-voting-option,78273 |newspaper=[[Warwick Beacon]] |date=January 10, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> Block was among those who testified before the Rhode Island Senate in a judiciary hearing on the elimination of the master lever in March 2012.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://oceanstatecurrent.com/liveblog/030612-ri-senate-judiciary-hearing/ |title=03/06/12 – RI Senate Judiciary Hearing |author=Katz, Justin |date=March 6, 2012 |website=[[Ocean State Current]] |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref>

A later study conducted by the Moderate Party, which examined ballots from the 2010 election in [[Burrillville, Rhode Island]], indicated that voters did not understand how to use the master lever. Voters that chose to vote for all Democratic candidates would often cancel out their votes when continuing to cast individual votes further down in the ballot.<ref name="GoLocalProv">{{cite web |url=http://www.golocalprov.com/politics/ri-taxpayers-urges-passage-of-bill-to-eliminate-master-lever/ |title=RI Taxpayers Urges Passage of Bill to Eliminate Master Lever |date=March 13, 2013 |website=GoLocalProv.com |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://thefederalhillgazette.com/5948/rhode-island-is-a-one-party-state-democratic/ |title=Rhode Island is a One Party State – Democratic |author=White, John Hazen Jr. |work=Federal Hill Gazette |date=March 20, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> Block cited the Moderate Party's campaign in the November 2012 election as an example with, he said, over 9,000 Rhode Island voters using the Moderate Party master lever mechanism despite not having a Moderate candidate on the ballot.<ref name="Kalunian-ML"/><ref>{{cite interview |last=Block |first=Ken |interviewer=John Carlevale (host) |title=Interview with Ken Block |url=http://vimeo.com/channels/365354/58209460 |publisher =Cox Communications |location=Providence |date=January 24, 2013 |work=State of the State |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> Politifact judged this claim as 'mostly true" saying that there were slightly ''under'' 9,000 such votes.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.politifact.com/rhode-island/statements/2013/feb/22/kenneth-block/block-says-moderate-party-has-lots-votes/ |title=Block says 9,000 R.I. voters used master lever for Moderate Party -- with no party candidates on ballot |author=McKinney, Michael |date=February 22, 2013 |website=PolitiFact.com |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref>

Another issue caused by the master lever, critics argue, is "under voting" at the local level, where many offices are officially non-partisan, with candidates being skipped on the ballot because they do not belong to a political party.<ref name="Perilli"/> The master lever also created an unfair advantage for majority political parties at the expense of minority and third parties.<ref>{{cite news |author=Achorn, Edward |date=May 26, 2009 |title=Drive for fair elections gains traction in R.I. |newspaper=The Providence Journal |quote=Worst of all, the master lever tends to give a huge advantage to members of the majority party, particularly well down the ballot, in races that would otherwise not draw as many votes. Independent and third-party candidates face a huge disadvantage.<br/>That is obviously one reason for the overwhelming dominance of one party in the General Assembly. Many seats go uncontested because potential opponents do not believe they have a fighting chance. They start a race, in effect, hundreds of votes behind because of the advantage the master lever provides the other guy. The system is inherently unfair, a racket. }}</ref> Both [[Allan Fung]] and [[Scott Avedisian]], Republican mayors of [[Cranston, Rhode Island|Cranston]] and [[Warwick, Rhode Island|Warwick]] respectively, agreed with this view; a ballot recount by Fung in his 2006 election revealed serious irregularities in votes cast by the master lever.<ref name="Kalunian-ML"/> In [[United States presidential election|U.S. presidential elections]], many voters unknowingly elect candidates simply by voting for a presidential nominee. An estimated 46,000 voters used the master lever during the 2010 gubernatorial elections, a number which doubled during the [[2012 United States presidential election|2012 U.S. presidential election]], giving local Democrats a huge advantage over non-Democratic candidates.<ref name="Kalunian-ML"/>

In January 2013, Block launched MasterLever.org to inform the public about the problems using the obsolete voting system in local and state elections. The website allowed supporters to send an automated letter to state officials including House Speaker [[Gordon D. Fox|Gordon Fox]], Senate President [[M. Teresa Paiva-Weed]], and Governor Lincoln Chafee. Within a week of its launch, 500 people had sent letters via the website in support the banning the master lever.<ref name="Kalunian-ML"/> That number rose to 2,600 within four months.<ref name="Mikolay"/> Although the majority of the General Assembly remained silent on the issue, Block's initiative eventually gained support from key political figures including Democratic Secretary of State [[Ralph Mollis]]<ref name="Smith"/><ref name="Perilli">{{cite web |url=http://www.brownpoliticsmemo.com/2013/01/09/ocean-state-pulling-the-master-lever/ |title=Ocean State: Pulling the master lever |author=Perilli, John |date=January 9, 2013 |website=BrownPoliticsMemo.com |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> and Governor Chafee.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ri.gov/press/view/18339 |title=Governor Lincoln D. Chafee Supports Elimination of 'Master Lever' |author=Office of the Governor |date=January 8, 2013 |website=RI.gov |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> Block polled legislators at the State House for their stance on the master lever which he then made available on the website.<ref name="Kalunian-ML"/> A ban on the master lever was also supported by RI-based government watchdog groups [[Common Cause]], [[Operation Clean Government]], and the Rhode Island Statewide Coalition.

Block made a second appearance on the April 5th edition of ''A Lively Experiment'' where he talked about his views on the master lever, [[payday loan]]s, and [[Tax credit#State tax credits|state tax credits]]. Block's report on waste and fraud in Rhode Island's state social service programs was also a topic of discussion on the show.<ref name="ALE2"/>

=== Involvement with RI Taxpayers ===
On February 4, 2013, Block succeeded Harriet Lloyd as president of the [[taxpayer groups|taxpayer advocacy group]] RI Taxpayers (formerly known as the Rhode Island Statewide Coalition).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://ripr.org/post/ken-block-takes-leadership-role-ri-taxpayers |title=Ken Block takes on leadership role with RI Taxpayers |author=Donnis, Ian |date=February 4, 2013 |website=RIPR.org |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.630wpro.com/common/page.php?pt=NEWS%3A+Taxpayer+advocates+announce+new+name%2C+leadership&id=4771&is_corp=0 |title=NEWS: Taxpayer advocates announce new name, leadership |author=Kalunian, Kim |date=February 4, 2013 |publisher=[[WPRO (AM)|WPRO]] |access-date=October 1, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140305013115/http://www.630wpro.com/common/page.php?pt=NEWS%3A+Taxpayer+advocates+announce+new+name%2C+leadership&id=4771&is_corp=0 |archive-date=March 5, 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The decision was discussed a week later on ''A Lively Experiment''; one of the guest panelists, columnist Donna Perry, was the executive director of RI Taxpayers.<ref>{{Cite episode |title=A Lively Experiment |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DrBGawEHKUQ#t=17m58s |access-date=October 1, 2012 |first=Dyana |last=Koelsch |network=PBS |station=WSBE-TV |date=August 10, 2012 }}</ref>

As president of the organization, Block was an active public speaker and made numerous appearances before state committee hearings. On March 13, 2013, he testified before RI House Judiciary Committee on behalf of both the Moderate Party and RI Taxpayers urging the members to pass legislation for eliminating "master lever" voting in the state.<ref name="GoLocalProv"/> At the RI Taxpayers' annual meeting on May 11, Block issued a formal challenge to the General Assembly to "stop stalling" on economic-related legislation for businesses and taxpayers before adjourning next month.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.golocalprov.com/politics/block-ri-taxpayers-organization-challenge-general-assembly-on-economy/ |title=Block/RI Taxpayers Organization Challenge General Assembly on Economy |date=May 10, 2013 |website=GoLocalProv.com |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> At another meeting, Block called for more citizen involvement in local government and warned that [[voter apathy]], especially among people under 40, would continue to result in poor legislation and a lack of accountability by political leadership.<ref>{{cite news |title=Block calls for more citizen involvement |author=Baron, Jim |url=http://www.woonsocketcall.com/node/8225 |newspaper=[[The Call (Woonsocket)|Woonsocket Call]] |date=May 12, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> He also spoke about the importance of economic reforms and the need to improve the state's business climate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://rhodybeat.com/stories/Flawed-RI-business-brand-critiqued-by-RI-Taxpayers-group,82012 |title='Flawed RI business brand' critiqued by RI Taxpayers group |author=Howell, John |date=May 14, 2013 |website=Rhodybeat.com |access-date=October 1, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304025019/http://rhodybeat.com/stories/Flawed-RI-business-brand-critiqued-by-RI-Taxpayers-group%2C82012 |archive-date=March 4, 2016 }}</ref> After a four-month term, Block resigned his position with RI Taxpayers to run in the [[Rhode Island gubernatorial election, 2014|2014 elections]].<ref name="Fitzpatrick">{{cite web |url=https://mobile.twitter.com/FitzProv/status/336857339275640832?screen_name=FitzProv |title=RI Taxpayers says Ken Block formally resigned as the group's president late last week. Block announced today he's running for governor. |author=Fitzpatrick, Edward |date=May 21, 2013 |website=[[Twitter.com]] |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref>

=== 2014 election ===
{{main|2014 Rhode Island gubernatorial election}}

On May 21, 2013, Block announced he would once again run for office as Governor of Rhode Island in the [[Rhode Island gubernatorial election, 2010|2014 election]].<ref name="Nagle" /><ref name="Fitzpatrick" /> He based his campaign on a plan to save Rhode Island taxpayers $1 billion by eliminating wasteful spending. Weeks after Block's campaign launch, political operative Jeff Britt joined the Moderate Party as Block's [[campaign manager]]; Britt was previously a political ally of Democratic House Speaker [[Gordon Fox]] and former Republican governor [[Don Carcieri]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://ripr.org/post/jeff-britt-manage-ken-blocks-campaign-governor |title=Jeff Britt to Manage Ken Block's Campaign for Governor |author=Donnis, Ian |date=July 22, 2013 |website=RIPR.org |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> His campaign was endorsed by blogger and third-party activist [[Dennis Mikolay]] praising him as a [[List of consumer organizations|government watchdog]].<ref name="Mikolay" /> One of his last public appearances as a Moderate candidate took place at [[Roger Williams University]] on September 30, 2013.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://pdq.rwu.edu/events/meet-and-greet-ken-block |title=University Events: Meet and Greet with Ken Block |author=Roger Williams University |website=RWU.edu |access-date=October 1, 2013 |author-link=Roger Williams University |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140305070845/http://pdq.rwu.edu/events/meet-and-greet-ken-block |archive-date=March 5, 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> 

==== Switch to the Republican Party ====
On October 28, 2013, Block officially announced at a [[Barrington, Rhode Island|Barrington]] [[town hall meeting]] that he would be joining the Republican Party with the intention of running as its candidate for the [[Rhode Island gubernatorial election, 2014|2014 gubernatorial election]].<ref name="Bickford" /><ref name="RSP" /><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.turnto10.com/story/23804926/block-to-announce-race-for-governor-as-republican |title=Block enters race for governor as Republican |agency=Associated Press |date=October 28, 2013 |website=[[WJAR|Turnto10.com]] |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> The news was not totally unexpected as Block, who had been largely funding the Moderate Party with his own finances,<ref name="Smith" /><ref name="Marcelo" /> including his 2010 campaign, commissioned a poll on his chances running as a Moderate or Republican candidate over the summer.<ref name="Gregg" /> A [[Brown University Graduate School|Taubman Center for Public Policy and American Institutions]] poll indicated only 9% of participants would vote for Block as a Moderate candidate.<ref name="Bashkatova" /> Three months earlier, Block suggested that he would be open to running as a Republican<ref>{{cite news |title=Lincoln Chafee's exit kick-starts R.I. governor's race |author=Klepper, David |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2013/09/05/chafee-exit-kick-starts-governor-race/q8OCl3Coxln1lOy2pCmPSM/story.html |newspaper=[[Boston Globe]] |date=September 6, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013 |archive-date=March 1, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140301162322/http://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2013/09/05/chafee-exit-kick-starts-governor-race/q8OCl3Coxln1lOy2pCmPSM/story.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> during a [[WPRO (AM)|WPRO]] interview with [[Buddy Cianci]]. He had also been courted by Republican [[Joe Trillo]] to join the party partially to prevent another four-way race which allowed then independent candidate Lincoln Chafee to win the 2010 election.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://oceanstatecurrent.com/analysis/ken-block-a-republican/ |title=Ken Block a Republican? |author=Chartier, Monique |date=July 25, 2013 |website=[[Ocean State Current]] |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref>

[[John DePetro]] called Block's decision a "smart political move" and speculated on the upcoming GOP primary between Block and Republican party favorite [[Cranston, Rhode Island|Cranston]] mayor [[Allan Fung]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.depetro.com/2013/10/will-ken-block-fung.html |title=Will Ken Block Fung? |author=DePetro, John |date=October 28, 2013 |website=Depetro.com |access-date=October 1, 2013 |author-link=John DePetro |archive-date=March 5, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140305190846/http://www.depetro.com/2013/10/will-ken-block-fung.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> The news was also discussed on ''A Lively Experiment'' with guest panelists Dave Layman, [[WPRO (AM)|WPRO]]/[[WPRI]] legal analyst Louis Pulner, [[Brown University]] [[political science]] professor [[Wendy Schiller]], and Arlene Violet.<ref>{{Cite episode |title=A Lively Experiment |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mOL2usaVCfU#t=11m52s |access-date=October 1, 2012 |first=Jim (host) |last=Hummel |network=PBS |station=WSBE-TV |location=Providence, Rhode Island |date=November 1, 2013 }}</ref>

Block declared his intention to run as the party's nominee in a letter to 20,000 Republican voters and based his campaign on an ambitious plan to save the state $1 billion in government waste over a four-year period.<ref name="Nagle"/><ref name="DeQuattro"/> He further explained that this amount could easily be achieved by focusing on "off budget" spending such as public sector retiree healthcare, temporary disability, and unemployment insurance. Block offered the state's Temporary Disability Insurance (TDI) program as an example. Cutting the annual costs in half for the TDI program, which is twice as much to run as [[New Jersey]], would save $80 million alone.<ref name="Gregg"/><ref name="Baron"/><ref name="RSP"/> Block outlined his plan on his official website in addition to other forums as well.<ref name="Bickford"/> In November 2013, he was invited to write a column for ''[[The Providence Journal]]'' to make his case for saving the state $1 billion by eliminating government waste.<ref>{{cite news|title=Ken Block: I can save R.I. taxpayers $1 billion |author=Block, Ken |url=http://www.providencejournal.com/opinion/commentary/20131113-ken-block-i-can-save-r.i.-taxpayers-1-billion.ece |newspaper=The Providence Journal |date=November 13, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140531123901/http://www.providencejournal.com/opinion/commentary/20131113-ken-block-i-can-save-r.i.-taxpayers-1-billion.ece |archive-date=May 31, 2014 }}</ref> Block was also interviewed by Tim White and Ted Nesi on the November 15 edition of WPRI's ''Eyewitness News'' and further explained his plan to save the state $1 billion.<ref name="WPRI-Newsmakers"/>

That same month, [[PolitiFact.com]] rated a claim by Block that Rhode Island has the most expensive unemployment system in the country as "false". According to the "2014 State Business Tax Climate Index", published by the [[Tax Foundation]], Rhode Island ranked 46 out of the 50 states. In the report, Rhode Island's unemployment insurance system was rated the worst in the country, however, that alone did not indicate it was the most expensive. Block's policy director, Matthew Schweich, defended the statement pointing out that Rhode Island was ranked near the bottom in almost every category and argued that the cumulative effect supports their claim.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.politifact.com/rhode-island/statements/2013/nov/17/kenneth-block/ken-block-says-rhode-islands-unemployment-insuranc/ |title=Ken Block says Rhode Island's unemployment insurance system is the most expensive in the U.S. |author=Emery, C. Eugene Jr. |date=November 17, 2013 |website=PolitiFact.com |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref> Block responded to the website in a [[letter to the editor]] of ''The Providence Journal'' acknowledging the error "but choosing the wrong words and intentionally misleading voters are different types of mistakes".<ref>{{cite news|title=Don't overlook the real problem R.I. faces |author=Block, Ken |url=http://blockforgovernor.com/news/kbfg_news.php?id=6&cat_id=&p= |archive-url=https://archive.today/20140228013502/http://blockforgovernor.com/news/kbfg_news.php?id=6&cat_id=&p= |url-status=dead |archive-date=February 28, 2014 |newspaper=The Providence Journal |date=December 4, 2013 |access-date=October 1, 2013 }}</ref>

In December 2013, longtime Rhode Island GOP fundraiser Tony Bucci resigned his slot on Fung's campaign committee to join Ken Block's gubernatorial campaign as a senior adviser and finance chairman.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://ripr.org/post/gop-fundraiser-tony-bucci-joins-block-campaign-fung-returns-fire |title=GOP Fundraiser Tony Bucci Joins Block Campaign; Fung Returns Fire |author=Donnis, Ian |date=December 9, 2013 |website=RIPR.org |access-date=October 1, 2013}}</ref>

Block lost the primary to Fung, 55% to 45%.

=== 2026 election ===
{{main|2026 Rhode Island gubernatorial election}}
Block, who had left the Republican Party to become an [[Independent politician|independent]] in 2020, announced a third bid for Governor of Rhode Island on April 2, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Crandall |first=Brian |date=2026-04-02 |title=Ken Block drops into Rhode Island's governor's race as independent |url=https://turnto10.com/politics/ken-block-drops-into-rhode-island-governor-race-independent-democrats-republicans-april-2-2026 |access-date=2026-04-23 |website=WJAR |language=en}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Block lives in [[Barrington, Rhode Island]] with his wife Jennifer, a middle school science teacher, and their two children, Anna and Sam.<ref name="Benton"/><ref name="Baron"/><ref name="Little"/>

